data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_063d3393a5ff0440006a73968e7f2c819091fdab1f43303fc6","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":371,"text":"As of the latest Bing-grounded results, the newest Perseverance rover news is that **NASA’s Perseverance captured Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos** — the first time humanity has observed Earth being occulted by another object from the surface of another planet. The image sequence was taken by Perseverance’s **Mastcam-Z** on **July 2, 2026** — mission sol 1,907 — and NASA/JPL published the story on **Aug. 5, 2026**【6:0†source】.\n\nKey details:\n\n- The images show **Earth as a tiny bright dot** moving across the Martian evening sky while **Phobos passes in front of it**, briefly making Earth “wink out” behind the moon【6:0†source】.\n- Perseverance took the observation from the **rim of Jezero Crater**, where it is continuing its extended science campaign【6:0†source】.\n- NASA said this required both planning and luck: Phobos crosses the Martian sky three times a day, and Earth is visible for months at a time, but aligning the two from the rover’s location is uncommon【6:0†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include its work at a region called **“Broom Point,”** where the rover has been examining ancient layered bedrock on Jezero Crater’s rim. NASA reported in July 2026 that Perseverance found evidence that a roughly **245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick** stack of rock there was formed by repeated ancient asteroid impacts【6:1†source】.\n\nPerseverance remains an **active mission** focused on searching for signs of ancient microbial life, studying Jezero Crater’s geology, and collecting Martian rock and regolith samples that could be returned to Earth by a future mission【6:3†source】."}

